# ISP-probe-why -- when a netwatch probe goes down, re-test it once from the
# router and log WHY it failed.
#
# Netwatch reports that a tcp-conn probe is down, never why. The difference
# matters: a timeout means packets are being lost on the path, which is the
# link; a refused or reset connection means the target is actively rejecting
# us, which is what rate limiting looks like. A second prober elsewhere on the
# LAN cannot answer this -- it starts from a different device, through the
# forward chain, NAT and the queues -- so the re-test runs here, from the
# router, and follows the same destination pin to the same ISP.
#
# Deliberately NOT part of ISP-control. A re-test blocks for up to its timeout,
# and during a total outage nine of them would stall the controller for half
# a minute -- delaying the very failover the probes exist to trigger. This runs
# on its own scheduler, and nothing here feeds back into routing.
#
# Each down event is re-tested exactly once: a probe is keyed by its name and
# the `since` timestamp of its current state, and marked BEFORE the re-test so
# an overlapping run started by the next tick skips it. At most three re-tests
# per run, so one run never blocks for longer than about nine seconds.
#
# Output, one line per down event, with fetch's own error text kept:
#   ... retest failed, timeout: failure: timeout connecting
#   ... retest failed, refused: failure: Connection refused
#   ... retest failed, no route: failure: Network unreachable
#   ... retest connected
#   ... retest connected (<error>)
# A timeout, a refusal or a reset means the TCP connection failed; "unreachable"
# means it was never attempted. Those strings were checked against fetch on this
# router. Note the case of the rest: an HTTP answer arrives as "failure: Status
# 308, Permanent Redirect", capital S, and RouterOS matching is case-sensitive,
# so matching on "status" to detect it would have filed every redirect as a
# failed connection. Any error that came AFTER the connection was made (a TLS
# problem, an HTTP error page) counts as connected: the path worked a moment
# after netwatch said it did not.
#
# The unreachable case was added on 2026-09-28, and it is the reason this script
# had been reporting the opposite of the truth. "Network unreachable" and "Host
# is unreachable" are raised at route lookup, before a packet leaves -- yet they
# fell through to the default and were logged as "retest connected" roughly two
# hundred times in one day. They are not a minor error class here: the pins use
# lookup-only-in-table, which has no fallthrough, so "Network unreachable" means
# that ISP's route went INACTIVE, which means its CPE stopped answering. That is
# the single most diagnostic line this script can emit.

:global ispWhySeen
:if ([:typeof $ispWhySeen] = "nothing") do={ :set ispWhySeen [:toarray ""] }

:local budget 3
# Only probes that are down AND have failed at least one test. A probe that
# was just created, or has not run since a reboot, is not "up" either -- on
# 2026-09-27 the three new Cloudflare probes were each re-tested seconds after
# the apply that created them, one with an empty `since`. Those are not
# failures and must not look like them.
:foreach n in=[/tool/netwatch/find where comment~"-probe\$" and status="down"] do={
  :if (($budget > 0) && ([/tool/netwatch/get $n failed-tests] > 0)) do={
    :local name [/tool/netwatch/get $n name]
    :local host [/tool/netwatch/get $n host]
    :local since [:tostr [/tool/netwatch/get $n since]]
    :if (($ispWhySeen->$name) != $since) do={
      :set ($ispWhySeen->$name) $since
      :set budget ($budget - 1)
      # HTTPS without certificate checks: only the connection matters here.
      :local result "retest connected"
      :onerror e in={
        /tool/fetch url=("https://" . $host . "/") check-certificate=no keep-result=no idle-timeout=3s
      } do={
        :set result ("retest connected (" . $e . ")")
        :if ($e~"timeout") do={ :set result ("retest failed, timeout: " . $e) }
        :if ($e~"refused") do={ :set result ("retest failed, refused: " . $e) }
        :if ($e~"reset") do={ :set result ("retest failed, reset: " . $e) }
        # Covers both "Network unreachable" and "Host is unreachable"; both are
        # lowercase in fetch's output and RouterOS matching is case-sensitive.
        :if ($e~"unreachable") do={ :set result ("retest failed, no route: " . $e) }
      }
      :log warning ("ISP-probe-why " . $name . " down since " . $since . ": " . $result)
    }
  }
}
